<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!-- SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT -->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <!-- :engine's AAR already declares INTERNET (it binds the loopback origin).
         Declared here too so the demo's requirement is visible in :app. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />

    <!-- Do Not Disturb access for the phone-permissions page's 专注模式 row.
         `protectionLevel=normal`, so it needs no runtime prompt — but without the
         declaration the app never appears in the system's "Do Not Disturb access"
         list, and the row's 去系统设置 lands on a page with no switch for it
         (measured 2026-11-01). The grant is still the user's: the row routes to
         that system page and reads `isNotificationPolicyAccessGranted` back. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_NOTIFICATION_POLICY" />

    <!--
      The background surface is NOT declared here, and must not be: it belongs to
      :dsh, whose library manifest declares the two services, the two receivers and
      the six permissions (FOREGROUND_SERVICE, FOREGROUND_SERVICE_DATA_SYNC,
      RUN_USER_INITIATED_JOBS, WAKE_LOCK, POST_NOTIFICATIONS,
      RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED). :app depends on :dsh, so the manifest merger puts
      them in the APK.

      Reading this file alone therefore shows zero services and zero receivers.
      That is a property of the source layout, not of the installed app. Read the
      merged artifact instead, under
      android/app/build/intermediates/merged_manifests/debug/processDebugManifest/,
      or, on a device, `adb shell dumpsys package com.phonedsh.probe` and look for
      TurnForegroundService / TurnJobService / TurnStopReceiver / BootReceiver.

      Declaring them again here would merge cleanly today and become a second
      source of truth tomorrow, so the app reads the merged manifest back at
      runtime instead (HostBackground.declaration) and reports the verdict on the
      runtime page's 前台服务可用性 row.
    -->

    <!--
      Backup policy (security checklist S5, plan R11):
        * android:allowBackup="false" is the primary switch: no cloud backup and
          no device-to-device transfer at all.
        * the two rule files are the second layer, required so that sensitive
          paths stay out if backup is ever enabled. API 31+ reads only
          dataExtractionRules; API 23-30 reads only fullBackupContent. Both are
          declared so neither API era silently falls back to "back everything up".
      The rule files exclude the K3 domain database and its -wal/-shm/-journal,
      the WAL workspace, the materialized worker bundle, and the SecureStore
      ciphertext directory (see their comments for the code path behind each).
    -->
    <!--
      Back gesture (`spec/ui/v1/README.md` §5 back-gesture, `platform.predictive-back`):
      the system owns the gesture and the page stack pops one level per gesture, so the
      app opts in here. `android:enableOnBackInvokedCallback` is API 33+ only (the
      platform's OnBackInvokedDispatcher); on older devices it is ignored and back
      arrives through the legacy `onBackPressed()` path, which the Activity's dispatcher
      routes to the same callback.

      The app's one back chain lives in `HostShell` (`PdshBackHandler`): drawer, then the
      top route's own inner level, then the route stack. The chain is deliberately
      *disabled* at the root route, so the platform's default (finish the Activity) runs
      there — printing `finish()` from a callback would take the task away from the
      system's predictive animation and from multi-window/recents semantics.

      This attribute only declares the intent: whether the platform carries back through
      the new path is the device's answer, which is why the 返回手势 row is measured at
      runtime rather than printed from this file.
    -->
    <!--
      Product identity: the label the launcher shows and the icons it draws. The
      APK's `applicationId` (`com.phonedsh.app`, set in build.gradle.kts) is what
      the system knows this app by; this file's component names are therefore
      fully qualified rather than `.MainActivity`, because a leading dot resolves
      against the applicationId and the class actually lives in the `namespace`
      package `com.phonedsh.probe`.

      Both icons are adaptive (API 26+ only, and minSdk is 26): the
      `mipmap-anydpi-v26` XMLs are the only icon resources, and `roundIcon`
      points at the same layers so a round-masked launcher gets the same mark
      from its own mask instead of a scaled bitmap.

      extractNativeLibs: `true`, because the Termux proot binaries and the Rust
      JNI library are executed from `applicationInfo.nativeLibraryDir`; Android
      10+ refuses `execve` on app-home files. The cost is that every packaged
      `.so` is materialized in app data (the 32 MB JNI library included), and
      the 16 KB gate runs in its extracted mode: ELF PT_LOAD alignment is still
      checked, while zip compression/offset no longer describe the executed
      file. `noCompress += "so"` and `keepDebugSymbols` in `:app` keep the
      packaged bytes equal to the pinned asset SHAs.

      allowBackup: already `false`, and it stays `false`. This app owns an API
      key's encrypted record under `noBackupFilesDir` plus the WAL workspace; a
      backup that carried either off the device would be a leak. The two rule
      files remain as the second layer for the day someone flips the switch.
    -->
    <application
        android:allowBackup="false"
        android:extractNativeLibs="true"
        android:dataExtractionRules="@xml/data_extraction_rules"
        android:enableOnBackInvokedCallback="true"
        android:fullBackupContent="@xml/backup_rules"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:label="PDSH"
        android:networkSecurityConfig="@xml/network_security_config"
        android:roundIcon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_round"
        android:supportsRtl="true"
        android:theme="@android:style/Theme.Material.Light.NoActionBar">

        <!--
          What the APK says about its own signature, readable without launching
          the app (XML comments cannot spell the tools' double-dash flags, so
          the exact command lines live in the "产品化" section of
          docs/receipts/android-port-pr-description.md):

            adb shell dumpsys package com.phonedsh.app | grep -A2 SIGNING
            aapt2 dump xmltree <apk> <the AndroidManifest.xml file flag>

          The values are manifest placeholders from build.gradle.kts, so they
          always describe the build they are in:
          `debug-keystore-fallback` + 调试签名，不可发布 when `release` fell back
          to AGP's debug key, `release-keystore` + 正式签名，可发布 otherwise.
          They are also exposed to code as BuildConfig.SIGNING_MODE /
          SIGNING_RELEASABLE / SIGNING_NOTICE, which is what the 关于 page reads.
        -->
        <meta-data
            android:name="com.phonedsh.app.SIGNING_MODE"
            android:value="${pdshSigningMode}" />
        <meta-data
            android:name="com.phonedsh.app.SIGNING_NOTICE"
            android:value="${pdshSigningNotice}" />

        <!-- 导出对话记录 writes a long Markdown document under cacheDir and hands
             it to the share sheet through this provider. Scope is exactly that
             one cache directory; the preview's provider keeps the workspace. -->
        <provider
            android:name="com.phonedsh.probe.TranscriptFileProvider"
            android:authorities="${applicationId}.pdsh.transcript"
            android:exported="false"
            android:grantUriPermissions="true">
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.support.FILE_PROVIDER_PATHS"
                android:resource="@xml/transcript_file_paths" />
        </provider>

        <activity
            android:name="com.phonedsh.probe.MainActivity"
            android:exported="true">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>
    </application>

</manifest>
